58
00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.891
Critical evaluation of sources is essential.

59
00:03:40.483 --> 00:03:43.507
Your turn. Say: Critical evaluation.

60
00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:47.751
Critical evaluation.

61
00:03:48.517 --> 00:03:49.634
Truth-seeking.

62
00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:53.509
Truth-seeking means trying to find what is true.

63
00:03:54.117 --> 00:03:57.161
Truth-seeking journalists ask hard questions.

64
00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.611
Your turn. Say: Truth-seeking.

65
00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:04.549
Truth-seeking.

66
00:04:05.333 --> 00:04:08.740
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

67
00:04:09.583 --> 00:04:12.245
Visal, what makes people trust the news?

68
00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.812
It is source verification that builds public trust.

69
00:04:17.917 --> 00:04:19.916
Why is press freedom important?

70
00:04:21.017 --> 00:04:24.166
It is free journalists who expose corruption.

71
00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.541
And what can we do?

72
00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:31.240
It is media literacy that protects us from disinformation.

73
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.133
Part three: grammar.

74
00:04:34.983 --> 00:04:38.214
Grammar focus: Emphatic structures (it is that).

75
00:04:38.514 --> 00:04:39.666
Look at the pattern.

76
00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.464
It is source verification that builds public trust.

77
00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:47.151
It is free journalists who expose corruption.

78
00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.560
It was a student who spotted the fake photo.

79
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:56.599
Put the most important idea after 'It is', then add 'that' (or 'who' for people) and the rest of the sentence.

80
00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:01.477
Be careful! Don't say: It is verification what builds trust.

81
00:05:03.433 --> 00:05:06.304
Say: It is verification that builds trust.

82
00:05:06.604 --> 00:05:08.626
Use 'It is … that' for emphasis.

83
00:05:09.433 --> 00:05:12.144
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

84
00:05:12.933 --> 00:05:14.143
Seeking the Truth.

85
00:05:14.443 --> 00:05:16.931
A healthy society needs press freedom.

86
00:05:17.933 --> 00:05:21.373
It is investigative journalism that uncovers hidden truths.

87
00:05:22.367 --> 00:05:25.237
Disinformation campaigns try to confuse the public.

88
00:05:26.233 --> 00:05:29.301
It is source verification that builds public trust.

89
00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.461
Media literacy skills help us separate fact from opinion.

90
00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:38.383
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

91
00:05:39.217 --> 00:05:41.890
It is verification blank builds trust.

92
00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:44.879
A, that. B, what. C, it.

93
00:05:48.433 --> 00:05:50.526
The answer is A: that.

94
00:05:51.683 --> 00:05:54.774
The right of journalists to report freely is press blank.

95
00:05:55.074 --> 00:05:58.774
A, freedom. B, trust. C, literacy.

96
00:06:02.333 --> 00:06:04.275
The answer is A: freedom.

97
00:06:05.417 --> 00:06:08.658
An organised effort to spread lies is a blank campaign.

98
00:06:08.958 --> 00:06:13.785
A, disinformation. B, truth-seeking. C, transparency.

99
00:06:17.333 --> 00:06:19.751
The answer is A: disinformation.

100
00:06:20.967 --> 00:06:24.049
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

101
00:06:24.883 --> 00:06:27.881
Why is press freedom important for a healthy society?

102
00:06:36.683 --> 00:06:38.218
Now listen to Visal.

103
00:06:38.518 --> 00:06:48.845
Press freedom is important because it is journalists who tell us the truth about leaders. Without it, people cannot make good decisions.

104
00:06:49.145 --> 00:06:50.878
Excellent answer, Visal!

105
00:06:51.783 --> 00:06:56.758
Great work! Now you can use twelve new words and emphatic structures (it is that).

106
00:06:57.058 --> 00:07:01.208
Today's value is truthfulness. Tell the truth and check the facts.

107
00:07:01.508 --> 00:07:04.183
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

108
00:07:04.483 --> 00:07:08.134
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

